5, -0.0019159317016601562, 0.0218963623046875, -0.03924560546875, 0.02935791015625, -0.0278472900390625, -0.0259857177734375, -0.01457977294921875, 0.0207366943359375, -0.0022411346435546875, -0.0125274658203125, -0.018951416015625, -0.018951416015625, -0.008056640625, -0.035858154296875, -0.00775146484375, 0.0259857177734375, -0.01177215576171875, 0.0256500244140625, -0.0220489501953125, 0.0616455078125, 0.004779815673828125, -0.020660400390625, 0.003894805908203125, -0.03497314453125, 0.0269927978515625, -0.0020503997802734375, -0.0157623291015625, 0.0087738037109375, -0.001529693603515625, -0.0016880035400390625, -0.0119781494140625, 0.029388427734375, -0.02569580078125, 0.0029296875, 0.00650787353515625, -0.031402587890625, -0.061065673828125, 0.0168914794921875, 0.005390167236328125, 0.0153045654296875, -0.0201263427734375, 0.0241546630859375, -0.06182861328125, 0.01343536376953125, -0.03131103515625, -0.01300048828125, -0.0212554931640625, -0.01776123046875, 0.0182037353515625, 0.024810791015625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Black holes are among the most fascinating and extreme objects studied in astronomy and astrophysics. A black hole is an astronomical body so compact that its gravity prevents anything, including light, from escaping. In the framework of Albert Einstein's theory of general relativity\u2014which describes gravitation as the bending or curvature of spacetime caused by mass and energy\u2014if a mass is compressed within a certain critical radius, it will form a black hole.\n\nThis critical radius is known as the Schwarzschild radius, and it specifically applies to non-rotating, uncharged black holes (called Schwarzschild black holes). Other types of black holes, such as rotating (Kerr) or charged (Reissner-Nordstr\u00f6m) black holes, have different properties and corresponding radii.\n\nThe boundary beyond which nothing can escape a black hole is called the event horizon. The event horizon is not a physical surface but rather a mathematically defined boundary in spacetime\u2014meaning it is \"theoretical\" in the sense that it marks the limit beyond which events cannot affect an outside observer. For an object falling into a black hole, crossing the event horizon produces no noticeable change locally; the object would not detect anything unusual at that precise moment. However, to a distant observer, the object appears to slow down and never quite crosses the horizon. Additionally, the strength of tidal forces\u2014differences in gravitational pull\u2014near the event horizon can vary depending on the black hole's mass; for stellar-mass black holes, these forces can be extreme, while for supermassive black holes, they may be relatively mild at the horizon.\n\nGeneral relativity also predicts that every black hole should contain a central singularity, a point where the curvature of spacetime becomes infinite and the known laws of physics break down. However, it is possible that quantum effects, such as those described by quantum gravity theories, could modify or resolve this singularity. For example, Hawking radiation\u2014a quantum mechanical process\u2014suggests that black holes can emit radiation and may eventually evaporate, potentially avoiding a true singularity.\n\nBlack holes serve as important laboratories for testing our understanding of gravity, quantum physics, and the fundamental laws of the universe. Observations of gravitational waves from black hole mergers, as well as studies of accretion disks and the motion of stars near black holes, provide crucial tests of general relativity in strong gravitational fields. These observations also offer direct evidence for the existence of black holes and help refine our understanding of their properties."}}